description:
A bundled dataset for the running example of the first lectures, with columns:
- late (binary target);
- delivery time (continuous target);
- distance (continuous);
- zone (categorical);
- two or three extra features for a multivariate exercise.
The target choice (late vs delivery time) is set by the preset. Sample size and Resample must work on it, so that students can see estimates move with n and from sample to sample.

Why: every first-class exercise (single probability, mean, sigmoid with a covariate, categorical zone, linear and logistic regression, softmax over zones or lateness classes) uses one story.

A preset can say which parts of the app are visible. New optional preset fields:
- an allow-list of palette entries and palette groups (pieces and ready-made groups);
- a way to hide or show UI regions and individual controls: the top-bar data controls (set, n, noise, standardise, resample, open CSV), file and preset menus, undo/redo, each results panel (fit, loss, surface, and the new panels from sibling beads), the equation strip, the canvas header (grain toggle, Fit, Tidy), and each training-bar control (Step, Run/Pause, Reset, Restore best, lr, optimizer, batch size, steps per frame, parameter count, step count).
Absent fields mean everything is shown, exactly as today. A preset with an empty palette allow-list shows no palette region at all (the canvas takes the room), likewise for the training bar.

Why: the first class starts with one locked parameter and almost nothing else on screen, and each later stage reveals more. Intended stages for the first two classes, as an illustration of what the mechanism must support:
(0) one locked parameter, a data strip, a score readout and a 1-D loss curve; no palette, no training bar;
(1) adds Step, Reset, step size and a tangent line; no Run;
(2) adds a palette with only Sigmoid and log loss, Run/Pause and the loss-over-steps chart;
(3) adds squared error and a histogram view;
(4) adds Input, Weight, Bias, a categorical input block, the fit plot and the 2-parameter loss surface;
(5) adds Standardize, several inputs, Linear, Softmax; later lessons add optimizers, batch size, activations and the rest.

General mechanism, not a beginner-only special case (epic tinker-6t7). It is an allow-list per preset that later lessons simply widen; do not add a 'beginner mode' flag. Every new panel or control added by later beads must get an id in this scheme.

Owner feedback: 'it's not clear what the sidebar does or how to add or rewire components.'

The left sidebar is the palette of 14 pieces. Today:
- Adding is by dragging a palette entry onto a wire (insert) or onto a piece of the same family (swap). Dropping anywhere else does nothing.
- Connecting is by dragging from a piece's small output dot to another piece.
- Rewiring an existing wire has no direct gesture. You connect a new wire into the port, which replaces the old one, or delete the wire.
None of this is signposted.

Work, within the CLAUDE.md rule that the tool contains no lesson text (labels, settings and one-sentence messages only):
- clear affordances: palette header/label, drag handles, hover and press states, drop-target highlighting while dragging (wires and pieces light up as valid targets), visible input/output ports with larger hit targets;
- a direct rewire gesture: drag a wire's end to another port, possibly coordinated with the 'drag everything' bead;
- tap-to-add as an alternative to drag (select a palette entry, then tap a wire);
- short tooltips/titles per palette entry (the piece name and what it connects to, one plain phrase);
- an empty or first-run state that makes the gestures discoverable without tutorial text.
Ask the owner if any guidance text beyond labels is wanted; that would be an exception to the no-lesson-text rule.

Write the bundled presets for the first two classes, one per stage, using the features from the sibling beads:
(0) one locked parameter, data strip, score readout (likelihood mode available), 1-D loss curve; no palette, no training bar;
(1) adds Step, Reset, step size and the tangent line; no Run; initial value fixed so hand calculations match;
(2) adds a palette with only Sigmoid and log loss, Run/Pause, the loss-over-steps chart;
(3) adds squared error and the histogram view;
(4) adds Input, Weight, Bias, the categorical input block, the fit plot and the 2-parameter loss surface;
(5) adds Standardize, several inputs, Linear, Softmax.
All on the delivery dataset. Each preset carries its own prompt or instructions and labels (lesson text lives in the preset, not the tool).
